x^{2n} + 1 + (x + 1)^{2n} \equiv 0 \pmod{x^2 + x + 1}.

We notice that the roots of $x^2 + x + 1$ are the cube roots of unity

\begin{align*}
\omega &= \frac{-1 + i\sqrt{3}}{2} \\
\bar{\omega} &= \frac{-1 - i\sqrt{3}}{2}.
\end{align*}

Then for the cube roots of unity (on the unit circle with radius $1$), the points $\omega$ and $\bar{\omega}$ are

\begin{align*}
\omega &= 1 \left( \cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{3}\right) + i\sin\left(\frac{2\pi}{3}\right) \right) \\
\bar{\omega} &= 1\left( \cos\left( -\frac{2\pi}{3} \right) + i\sin\left( -\frac{2\pi}{3} \right) \right)
\end{align*}

Now we observe the condition $x^{2n} + 1 + (x + 1)^{2n} \equiv 0 \pmod{x^2 + x + 1}.$ If we plug in the cube root of unity $\omega,$:

\begin{align*}
\omega^{2n} + 1 + (\omega + 1)^{2n} &= 0 \\
\left( \omega^{2n} + 1 \right) + \left( -\bar{\omega} \right)^{2n} &= 0
\end{align*}

